romans they existed then some stuff  happened and now they don't  and the people who live in rome's former  heartlands are now called italians but  when did this change occur  when did the romans become italians now  the answer to this question is dependent  on what your definition of roman is  is it a person living in the roman  empire people identifying themselves as  roman  speaking latin or upholding the ideals  of the roman empire  if it's living in the roman empire then  for the people in what's now italy it  was roughly 476 that it all came to an  end  sort of you see despite them being a  part of the brand new kingdom of italy  the first one that is  they still had a roman senate in fact  they still had the roman senate would  have spoken latin and were officially a  client state of what was left of the  roman empire in the east  many who lived there still considered  themselves to be romans and odawasa  italy's new ruler referred to himself as  being king of both romans and goths  odd oasis successors mostly used the  same terminology but it wasn't long  until emperor justinian and his trusty  general belisarius came knocking and  reconquered italy on behalf of  constantinople  they held onto it all for about seven  minutes before the lombards turned up  and conquered some and then a lot of the  territory of italy  importantly the eastern roman empire  still held on to some lands there which  started the long-running political  divisions of the italian peninsula  however in the territories ruled by the  lombards the institutions of the roman  empire notably the senate were abolished  and so began some good old-fashioned  absolute feudal monarchy  that said the idolization of rome  persisted and roman law remained  dominant  after this the process of change was a  slow one the lombards maintained control  over much of italy despite making up  about five percent of its population for  over two centuries  and the kings there soon stopped  referring to their subjects as romans  but as lombards  whilst at first both roman and germanic  law was used over time germanic law  became the dominant one  whereas in the parts ruled by byzantium  greek became the language of government  but the people there were still  considered to be romans  that said there were cultural  differences now between the byzantine  romans and their subjects living in  italy  when religious fervor led to the  empire-wide iconoclasm the destruction  of images on religious grounds the  romans living in italy didn't bother  when the franks turned up in the eighth  century and conquered most of the  peninsula the people living there were  not considered to be romans by most  outsiders  latin was still the primary language of  the region and over the next four  centuries it slowly morphed into  medieval italian  so with rome done and its identity a  thing of the past when did the people in  italy become italians  well regional identities separated along  political lines  venetians florentines neapolitans and of  course romans  but in the sense of living in and around  the city of rome not being literal  citizens of the roman empire  it wasn't until the renaissance that the  idea of the people who lived in italy  all being italians in a much deeper  sense started to pop  up this process was a very slow one but  it took off after the napoleonic wars  this was when the people living in the  italian peninsula got fed up of all  these foreign powers ruling over them  and fighting their wars there and as  such writers and activists started to  define what an italian was and how to  build them a nation  yet even after italy was born many of  the people who lived there still saw  themselves identify first and foremost  by the region of their birth  but for most of the people living in the  italian peninsula they were italians no  longer romans proper but the successes  and bearers of their legacy  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a sp